      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to follow the rules of the game and track the numbers removed by Carolyn. The initial list of integers is \\([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\\). On her first turn, Carolyn removes the number \\(2\\). This leaves the list \\([1, 3, 4, 5, 6]\\).\n\nFor each turn, Carolyn must remove a number that has at least one positive divisor other than itself remaining in the list. After removing \\(2\\), the remaining numbers are \\(1, 3, 4, 5, 6\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine which of these numbers are not divisors of any previous removed numbers. The removed number \\(2\\) was a prime number, and it is not a divisor of any other number in the list so far. Therefore, the next number Carolyn can remove is \\(1\\). This is because \\(1\\) is a divisor of every number, and neither \\(3, 4, 5,\\) or \\(6\\) have \\(1\\) as a divisor.\n\nSo, Carolyn removes \\(1\\). The list is now \\([3, 4, 5, 6]\\).\n\nNext, we need to ensure that the subsequent numbers Paul removes are the divisors of the number Carolyn just removed. Paul removes the number \\(3\\), leaving the list \\([4, 5, 6]\\). Now, Carolyn cannot remove \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) (since \\(1\\) and \\(3\\) are the only numbers left and they are divisors of \\(1\\) and \\(3\\)), and she cannot remove \\(2\\) (since \\(2\\) is not a divisor of any number in the list after her previous move).\n\nThus, Carolyn can only remove \\(4, 5,\\) or \\(6\\). Since \\(4\\) is the only number left and it is not a divisor of any smaller number in the list, Carolyn removes \\(4\\). The list is now \\([5, 6]\\).\n\nNext, Paul removes the number \\(5\\), leaving the list \\([6]\\). Carolyn cannot remove any number from the list, and Paul removes the remaining number \\(6\\).\n\nThe sequence of numbers removed by Carolyn is \\(2, 1, 4\\). The sum of these numbers is \\(2 + 1 + 4 = 7\\).\n\nThus, the sum of the numbers that Carolyn removes is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
